explorations/compile-ladder/rung-index-bound/REPORT.md:187:3. **The rule names `FortressLibrary.Index` by its qualified name** (fork 6): in the checker by the `Id`'s api and text, in walk by the identity of the library's `FType`, in the desugarer by the compilation unit's name and the trait's. Ways not taken: the bare text (the probes; a program's own `Index` would be read as the library's); `typeCons` identity in the checker (it throws for a name not in the trait table, as `CompilerLibrary.Index`); a `Types.INDEX` constant (it would need resetting per library). Evidence: `Types.java:44-70`; `TopLevelEnv.java:324-374`.
explorations/compile-ladder/rung-index-bound/REPORT.md:193:9. **A type that extends only `Index` extends `Object` too.** Ways not taken: the planner's line alone (`object Slot(n: ZZ32) extends Index end` would be no `Object`, lack `Object`'s methods, and break the traits chapter's rule that every trait but the three extends `Object`); a rule in walk only. Evidence: fork 8 asks programs with index types of their own to declare them `Index`; `TupleIndexBoundWalk`'s `Slot` assertions; the checker probe. The bare text `Index` is read here, since names are not resolved before disambiguation; a clause naming a program's own `Index`, already an `Object`, gains a redundant `Object` at worst.
explorations/compile-ladder/rung-index-bound/REPORT.md:199:15. **No compiled test.** Way not taken: a compiled test of a program's own `Index` (fork 6 makes the rule ignore it). Evidence: the prelude declares no `Index`.
explorations/compile-ladder/rung-index-bound/SKEPTIC.md:31:10. **Competing declarations.** No `.fss` or `.fsi` file of the tree outside `FortressLibrary` declares a type `Index`; in `ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/`, `indexTypeName` and the two `isLibraryIndex` and one `namesOnlyAnyOrIndex` are declared once each. A program's own `Index` is now refused under walk (section 3).
explorations/compile-ladder/rung-index-bound/SKEPTIC.md:48:- Decision 3 gives as its reason against the bare text that "a program's own `Index` would be read as the library's". Under walk a program cannot declare a type of its own named `Index` at all: the disambiguator refuses it as ambiguous with the library's, as it refuses one named `Indexed` (section 3). The qualified name matters on the compiled path and in the desugarer's reading; the decision stands.
explorations/compile-ladder/rung-index-bound/SKEPTIC.md:61:- **A program's own `trait Index`**, `object Slot(n: ZZ32) extends Index` and `takesObject(s)`: the old code 'PASS' under walk; the new code refuses it, 'Type name may refer to: Index, FortressLibrary.Index', as the old code refuses a program's own `trait Indexed`, 'Type name may refer to: Indexed, FortressLibrary.Indexed'. On the compiled path, old and new 'PASS' at one thread and four (the new run with a copy of the base build's caches for the compiler's library jars, which the rung's tree no longer held; the code generator is unchanged).
explorations/compile-ladder/rung-index-bound/SKEPTIC.md:89:**The homes.** Row 783: home 1 (`TupleIndexBoundWalk`, the full check). The void type: home 1 (my assertions). Row 805: home 3's row-only form, as the report says, since no gated test can hold it. Row 806: home 2 (`XXXIndexBesideTupleOverloadWalk`) and its row. Walk's stop at a broken `Index` bound: row 471, a note. A program's own `Index` refused under walk: no defect, the disambiguator's rule for every library type name. Row 161 (a user generic `opr +[\I\](a: Array[\RR64,I\], s: RR64)` under walk) stays true under walk; the checker now refuses its unbounded `I`, which the report's last point names for microGPT's helpers.
explorations/compile-ladder/rung-index-bound/SKEPTIC.md:110:- A program's own type named `Index` is refused under walk ('Type name may refer to: Index, FortressLibrary.Index'): the library's new trait name is reserved in every program, as every library type name is. No program of the tree declares one.

